WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.042 --> 00:00:07.750
MIGUEL HERNAN: Many interesting
things happened during the 1980s.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.330
One of those things was
the launching in 1983

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:14.360
of a large randomized trial on
folic acid and birth defects.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.950
This was the MRC vitamin
study, which included women

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.070
from 33 countries who were
planning to get pregnant.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:25.330
Amazingly, the risk of neural tube
birth defects, a type of birth defect

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:29.843
was reduced by 70% in the
group assigned to folic acid.

00:00:29.843 --> 00:00:32.259
And this was a big deal, because
neural tube birth defects

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:35.342
are serious birth defects that happen
when the central nervous system does

00:00:35.342 --> 00:00:37.810
not close in the first
28 days of pregnancy.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.160
And this lack of closure can lead to
very serious disabilities or death.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.220
So the findings from the trial
were used to support major changes

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:46.750
in public health policy.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.270
In the United States, the
Centers for Disease Control

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:53.920
advises all women to take than 0.4
milligrams of folic acid every day

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.270
before getting pregnant.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:58.930
And many countries have implemented
mandatory fortification of wheat flour

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:00.010
with folic acid.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:03.350
So women can get their daily dose
of folic acid from their food.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:07.720
However, the MRC vitamin trial
left some questions unanswered.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:11.140
First, this study only included
women with history of pregnancies

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:12.880
affected by neural tube defects.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.910
So would folic acid to work
for the rest of the women?

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.480
And second, the study gave
women high doses of folic acid.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.150
Would the same effect be seen
with the lower and possibly safer

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.990
doses that were usually included
in multi-vitamin supplements?

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:29.230
Since no randomized trials were
available to answer these questions,

00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:31.240
investigators did what they always do.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.300
They analyzed data from
observational studies.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.590
And one of those studies was the
Sloan Epidemiology Unit Birth Defects

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:41.080
Study at Boston University.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:45.250
This observational study included
pregnancies from several North American

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.120
cities in the 1990s.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.780
And this study estimated that
the risk of birth defects

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.710
was 35% lower among pregnancies
exposed to folic acid.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:56.950
35% lower, that's a strong association.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.920
Can we then conclude that
folic acid at the usual doses

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.940
lowers the risk of neural tube
defects by 35% in normal women?

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.370
Well, maybe more educated
women take more vitamins

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.220
and also, do other things that
reduce risk of birth defects.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:11.950
If that is the case,
then we would expect

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:15.270
to find a lower risk of birth
defects in folic acid users,

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:19.060
even if folic acid had no effect
on the risk of birth defects,

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.290
or it might be the other way around.

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:24.100
Maybe women with a history of
birth defects take more vitamins,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.670
and these women are at the higher risk
of having a baby with birth defects.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.070
If that were the case,
then our analysis would

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:33.670
underestimate the beneficial effects
of folic acid on birth defects.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.879
Folic acid might reduce
the risk by, say, 50%,

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:39.670
but because the high risk women
are more likely to take folic acid,

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:43.540
we only see a 35% lower risk.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:46.390
Like any observational
study, this one may

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.390
be affected by confounding,
which means that in our analysis,

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.260
we will need to adjust for
variables, like education

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.980
and prior history of birth defects.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:00.160
And in particular, it turns out that
there is a variable L in our dataset.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.140
And if we adjust for L, the
estimate moves from a 35% reduction

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.260
to only a 20% reduction in risk.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.860
That's a big change.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.260
So do we need to adjust for L then?

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:14.950
What is the closest to the
true effect of folic acid?

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:20.380
The unadjusted estimate of 35% lower
risk or the L adjusted estimate

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.480
of 20% lower risk?

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.260
Questions like this arise
constantly in research studies.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:29.800
And by the end of this lesson, you will
know how to handle these questions.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:32.590
You will be able to explain
under which circumstances

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.840
you would want to adjust
for the variable L

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:39.660
to quantify the effect of folic acid
on the risk of neural tube defects.

